export const passages = {
  
1: `\tConstantinople fell on May 29, 1453 when the Ottoman Empire under Sultan Mehmed II conquered the Byzantine Empire. This conquest closed the overland Silk Road and Red Sea trade routes between Europe and Asia. The Venetian and Genoese monopolies over Mediterranean ports forced western European powers to discover direct oceanic routes to India for spices, silk, and textiles.

\tPrince Henry the Navigator of Portugal financed extensive expeditions along the west coast of Africa. In 1488, the Portuguese explorer Bartolomeu Dias rounded the southern tip of Africa, naming it the Cape of Good Hope. Following this breakthrough, Vasco da Gama set sail from Lisbon on July 8, 1497 with four ships: the Sao Gabriel, the Sao Rafael, the Berrio, and a 200-ton supply vessel, commanding an expedition of approximately 170 men.

\tAfter rounding the Cape of Good Hope, Vasco da Gama sailed up the east African coast to Malindi in present-day Kenya. In Malindi, he hired an experienced Gujarati pilot named Abdul Majid, who guided the fleet across the Arabian Sea. Vasco da Gama arrived at the port of Kappad near Calicut on May 20, 1498. The Hindu ruler of Calicut, who held the hereditary title of the Zamorin, granted him an audience and allowed him to trade.

\tPedro Alvares Cabral commanded the second Portuguese voyage in 1500 and engaged in naval clashes with Arab merchants. Vasco da Gama returned to India in 1502 to establish fortified factories. Portugal founded its first factory at Cochin in 1503 and its second factory at Cannanore in 1505. Francisco de Almeida arrived in 1505 as the first Portuguese Viceroy in India, implementing the Blue Water Policy to secure naval supremacy over the Indian Ocean.

\tAlfonso de Albuquerque became Viceroy in 1509 and laid the territorial foundation of Portuguese rule. Albuquerque captured Goa from Sultan Ismail Adil Shah of Bijapur in November 1510, turning Goa into Portugal's administrative capital. In 1530, Governor Nino da Cunha officially moved the capital from Cochin to Goa. Portugal introduced tobacco, cashew nuts, and the first printing press to India at Goa in 1556.

\tThe Dutch East India Company, known as the VOC, was founded in 1602. The Dutch built their first Indian factory at Masulipatnam in 1605, followed by factories at Pulicat in 1610, Surat in 1616, and Chinsurah in 1653. The Dutch broke Portuguese naval dominance in the Spice Islands of Indonesia and captured Portuguese forts along the Malabar Coast. However, the Dutch power declined sharply after British forces under Robert Clive decisively defeated them at the Battle of Bedara in Bengal in November 1759.`,

2: `\tQueen Elizabeth I granted a royal charter to the English East India Company on December 31, 1600, securing a fifteen-year monopoly on English trade in the East Indies. Captain William Hawkins arrived at the court of Mughal Emperor Jahangir in 1609 aboard the ship Hector to request trading rights, but Portuguese interference blocked him. Captain Thomas Best defeated the Portuguese fleet at the Battle of Swally near Surat in November 1612, convincing Jahangir to permit an English factory at Surat in 1613. Sir Thomas Roe arrived in 1615 as King James I's ambassador, obtaining imperial farman rights to build factories across Mughal territories.

\tThe English established their first factory in South India at Masulipatnam in 1611. In 1639, Francis Day obtained land from the Raja of Chandragiri and constructed Fort St. George in Madras. King Charles II received Bombay as a dowry from Portugal in 1661 upon marrying Catherine of Braganza, leasing the island to the Company in 1668 for ten pounds annually. Job Charnock consolidated the three villages of Sutanuti, Gobindapur, and Kalikata in 1690, building Fort William around 1700 to form Calcutta. In 1717, Emperor Farrukhsiyar issued a farman, called the Magna Carta of the Company, granting duty-free trade in Bengal in exchange for a yearly tribute of three thousand rupees.

\tThe French East India Company was established in 1664 under King Louis XIV by finance minister Jean-Baptiste Colbert. The French set up their first Indian factory at Surat in 1668 through Caron and founded Pondicherry in 1674 under Francois Martin. Commercial rivalry between the British and French escalated into three Carnatic Wars between 1746 and 1763 along the Coromandel coast. The First Carnatic War (1746–1748) was an extension of the War of the Austrian Succession and ended with the Treaty of Aix-la-Chapelle, with French Governor Dupleix capturing Madras before returning it to the British. The Second Carnatic War (1749–1754) revolved around disputed successions in Hyderabad and Carnatic, resulting in the recall of Dupleix and the Treaty of Pondicherry. The Third Carnatic War (1758–1763) broke out with the Seven Years' War; British General Eyre Coote decisively defeated French commander Count de Lally at the Battle of Wandiwash on January 22, 1760. The 1763 Treaty of Paris reduced French possessions strictly to unfortified trading posts.

\tIn Bengal, Nawab Siraj-ud-Daulah clashed with the British over unauthorized fortification of Calcutta and misuse of trade permits (dastaks). Siraj-ud-Daulah captured Fort William on June 20, 1756, an event linked to the Black Hole tragedy. Robert Clive and Admiral Watson sailed from Madras to recapture Calcutta in January 1757, forcing the Nawab to sign the Treaty of Alinagar. Clive bribed the Nawab's commander-in-chief Mir Jafar, financier Jagat Seth, and merchant Rai Durlabh. On June 23, 1757, Clive's forces defeated Siraj-ud-Daulah at the Battle of Plassey on the banks of the Bhagirathi River. Siraj-ud-Daulah fled to Murshidabad and was executed by Mir Jafar's son Miran, inaugurating British political dominance over Bengal.`,

3: `\tFollowing the Battle of Plassey, the British installed Mir Jafar as the Nawab of Bengal in June 1757, exacting large financial concessions and territories including the 24 Parganas. When Mir Jafar failed to satisfy rising British revenue demands, the Company replaced him with his son-in-law Mir Qasim in 1760. Mir Qasim transferred the capital from Murshidabad to Munger in Bihar, reformed his army on European models, established a firearms foundry, and abolished all internal transit customs to place Indian merchants on equal footing with Company officials. This move provoked the British Council at Calcutta to depose him and reinstate Mir Jafar in 1763, sparking armed conflict.

\tMir Qasim fled to Awadh and forged a triple military alliance with Nawab Shuja-ud-Daulah of Awadh and the fugitive Mughal Emperor Shah Alam II. The allied army of approximately forty thousand troops marched toward Bengal but faced the British East India Company forces led by Major Hector Munro. On October 22, 1764, Munro's disciplined army of seven thousand men decisively defeated the combined allied forces at the Battle of Buxar on the banks of the Ganges River. While Plassey had been won primarily through palace intrigue and conspiracy, Buxar was a direct clash of arms that proved British military superiority and placed North India at the Company's disposal.

\tRobert Clive returned to India as Governor of Bengal in May 1765 and concluded the Treaty of Allahabad in August 1765. Under this treaty, Shuja-ud-Daulah surrendered the districts of Kora and Allahabad, agreed to pay a war indemnity of fifty lakh rupees, and entered a defensive alliance. Shah Alam II confirmed the Company's possession of Kora and Allahabad, received an annual imperial pension of twenty-six lakh rupees, and granted the Company the Diwani rights—the constitutional authority to collect revenue and administer civil justice—over the provinces of Bengal, Bihar, and Orissa.

\tClive introduced the Dual System of Government in Bengal in 1765. Under this framework, the Company held the Diwani (revenue and civil justice) while the Nawab retained the Nizamat (police and criminal administration), creating power without responsibility for the Company and responsibility without power for the Nawab. The system led to severe administrative breakdown, rampant corruption among private traders, and economic ruin, which directly intensified the Great Bengal Famine of 1770, wiping out one-third of the local population.

\tWarren Hastings was appointed Governor of Bengal in 1772 and abolished the Dual System, bringing revenue collection under direct British supervision. To check rampant financial abuses, the British Parliament enacted the Regulating Act of 1773, elevating the Governor of Bengal to the Governor-General of Bengal with a four-member executive council, making Hastings the first to hold the office. The act also established a Supreme Court of Judicature at Fort William in Calcutta in 1774, appointing Sir Elijah Impey as its first Chief Justice.`,

4: `\tThe Kingdom of Mysore emerged as a major challenger to British expansion in South India under Haidar Ali, who rose through the ranks of the Wodeyar army to seize de facto power in 1761. Haidar Ali established a modern arms factory at Dindigul with French technical assistance, reorganized his cavalry, and expanded his territory across the Malabar Coast. Alarmed by his growing influence, the British formed a coalition with the Nizam of Hyderabad and the Marathas, triggering four successive Anglo-Mysore Wars between 1767 and 1799.

\tThe First Anglo-Mysore War (1767–1769) began when British forces attacked Mysore. Haidar Ali outmaneuvered the British commanders, bought off the Nizam and Marathas, and marched his cavalry directly to the gates of Madras. Faced with imminent defeat, the British Council signed the humiliating Treaty of Madras in April 1769, establishing a mutual defensive pact where both sides agreed to restore conquered territories and assist each other if attacked by a third power.

\tThe Second Anglo-Mysore War (1780–1784) erupted when the British violated the Treaty of Madras by refusing to assist Haidar Ali against a Maratha invasion in 1771, followed by their capture of the French-controlled port of Mahe in Mysore's territory in 1779. Haidar Ali formed an alliance with the Nizam and Marathas, swept into the Carnatic, and captured Arcot. However, British General Sir Eyre Coote halted his advance and defeated Haidar Ali at the Battle of Porto Novo in July 1781. Haidar Ali died of cancer in December 1782, leaving the war to his son, Tipu Sultan. Tipu maintained fierce resistance until both sides signed the Treaty of Mangalore in March 1784 under Governor-General Warren Hastings, agreeing to an exchange of prisoners and status quo ante bellum.

\tTipu Sultan, known as the Tiger of Mysore, modernized his administration, introduced a new solar calendar, created new coinage, and developed military rocketry using iron-cased Mysorean rockets. He sought foreign alliances against the British by dispatching diplomatic embassies to the Ottoman Sultan, King Louis XVI of France, and Zaman Shah of Afghanistan, while joining the Jacobin Club and planting a Tree of Liberty at his capital, Srirangapatna.

\tThe Third Anglo-Mysore War (1790–1792) began when Tipu attacked the British ally Travancore over the control of Cranganore. Governor-General Lord Cornwallis allied with the Nizam and Marathas, invaded Mysore, and besieged Srirangapatna in 1792. Tipu was forced to accept the punitive Treaty of Srirangapatna in March 1792, surrendering half of his territory—including Malabar, Dindigul, and Baramahal—paying a war indemnity of over three crore rupees, and handing over two of his young sons as hostages.

\tThe Fourth Anglo-Mysore War occurred in 1799 under the aggressive expansionist policy of Governor-General Lord Wellesley. Wellesley demanded that Tipu break all French ties and sign the Subsidiary Alliance. Tipu rejected the ultimatum. British forces under General George Harris and Arthur Wellesley attacked Mysore simultaneously from Madras and Bombay. Tipu Sultan died defending the breach in the walls of Srirangapatna on May 4, 1799. The British annexed coastal districts, rewarded parts of the kingdom to the Nizam, and restored the truncated core of Mysore to a child heir of the Hindu Wodeyar dynasty under a strict Subsidiary Alliance treaty.`,

5: `\tThe Maratha Confederacy emerged as the primary contender to replace Mughal supremacy across northern and central India after the decline of Aurangzeb. Following their severe defeat by Ahmad Shah Abdali at the Third Battle of Panipat on January 14, 1761, the young Peshwa Madhavrao I worked to restore Maratha authority until his early death from tuberculosis in 1772. Internal factional struggles erupted when Raghunathrao murdered his nephew Narayanrao to claim the Peshwaship. Opposed by a council of twelve chieftains known as the Barbhai Council, led by the astute statesman Nana Fadnavis, Raghunathrao fled to Bombay and signed the Treaty of Surat with the British in 1775, triggering the First Anglo-Maratha War (1775–1782).

\tBritish forces under Colonel Keating suffered reverses against Maratha cavalry at Wadgaon in 1779, signing the Convention of Wadgaon to surrender occupied lands. Governor-General Warren Hastings rejected the convention and dispatched reinforcements under Colonel Goddard, who captured Ahmedabad and Bassein, while Captain Popham took Gwalior. The prolonged war ended with the Treaty of Salbai on May 17, 1782, mediated by Mahadji Shinde. The treaty guaranteed twenty years of peace, established Raghunathrao's young nephew Madhavrao II as Peshwa, gave Salsette to the British, and granted Raghunathrao a pension.

\tThe Maratha Confederacy split into five semi-autonomous centers: the Peshwa at Pune, the Gaekwad at Baroda, the Scindia (Shinde) at Gwalior, the Holkar at Indore, and the Bhonsle at Nagpur. When Nana Fadnavis died in March 1800, internal discipline broke down. The weak Peshwa Baji Rao II murdered Vithoji Holkar, prompting Yashwantrao Holkar to march on Pune and defeat the combined armies of the Peshwa and Daulat Rao Scindia at the Battle of Hadapsar in October 1802. Baji Rao II fled to the British outpost at Bassein and signed the Subsidiary Alliance under the Treaty of Bassein on December 31, 1802.

\tThe Second Anglo-Maratha War (1803–1805) began when Scindia and Bhonsle rejected the Treaty of Bassein as an insult to Maratha honor. British forces commanded by General Arthur Wellesley defeated the allied armies at the Battle of Assaye and Battle of Argaon in late 1803, while Lord Lake captured Aligarh and Delhi, defeating Scindia at Laswari. Bhonsle signed the Treaty of Deogaon in December 1803, surrendering Cuttack. Scindia signed the Treaty of Surji-Anjangaon in December 1803, surrendering Delhi, Agra, and parts of Bundelkhand. Yashwantrao Holkar entered the war late and inflicted tactical losses on British units under Monson, but eventually signed the Treaty of Rajghat in 1805 with Sir George Barlow.

\tThe Third Anglo-Maratha War (1817–1818) was provoked by Governor-General Lord Hastings' campaigns against the Pindaris, irregular cavalry operating from Maratha territories. Resentful of British meddling, Peshwa Baji Rao II attacked the British Residency at Pune in November 1817, while Appa Sahib Bhonsle struck at Nagpur and Malhar Rao Holkar II took up arms. British forces routed the Peshwa at the Battle of Khadki, Bhonsle at Sitabuldi, and Holkar at Mahidpur in December 1817. Baji Rao II surrendered in June 1818; the British abolished the office of the Peshwa, exiled him to Bithoor near Kanpur with an annual pension, annexed the Maratha core territories into the Bombay Presidency, and established a tiny protectorate at Satara under Pratap Singh, a direct descendant of Chhatrapati Shivaji.`,

6: `\tGovernor-General Lord Wellesley (1798–1805) expanded British rule using the Subsidiary Alliance system. Under this treaty, an Indian ruler surrendered control of foreign affairs, maintained a British military force within his borders at his own expense, expelled other Europeans, and accepted a British Resident at court. In return, the Company promised protection against internal rebellion and external aggression. The Nizam of Hyderabad signed first in 1798, followed by Mysore and Tanjore in 1799, Awadh in 1801, Peshwa Baji Rao II in 1802, Bhonsle in 1803, and Scindia in 1804. This system demilitarized native states and caused crippling debts.

\tIn northwest India, British fear of Russian expansion drove aggressive frontier conquests. Amirs of Sindh had signed friendship treaties in 1809 and 1832. During the First Anglo-Afghan War, Governor-General Lord Ellenborough sent Major-General Sir Charles Napier to enforce compliance. Napier defeated the Baluch forces of the Talpur Amirs at the Battle of Miani and Battle of Dubba in 1843, annexing Sindh to the Bombay Presidency in August 1843.

\tMaharaja Ranjit Singh united the twelve Sikh misls into the sovereign Sikh Empire, modernizing his forces into the Fauj-i-Khas. In April 1809, Ranjit Singh signed the Treaty of Amritsar with Charles Metcalfe, fixing the Sutlej River as the boundary with the Company. After Ranjit Singh died in 1839, political instability rocked Lahore. The First Anglo-Sikh War (1845–1846) began when Sikh troops crossed the Sutlej. After battles at Mudki, Ferozeshah, Aliwal, and Sobraon, Sir Hugh Gough forced the Treaty of Lahore in March 1846. The Company seized Jalandhar Doab, imposed a one-crore indemnity, and sold Kashmir to Gulab Singh under the Treaty of Amritsar. The Second Anglo-Sikh War (1848–1849) began with uprisings by Mulraj at Multan and Chattar Singh in Hazara. Following Chillianwala and a victory at Gujrat on February 21, 1849, Lord Dalhousie annexed Punjab on March 29, 1849, exiling Maharaja Duleep Singh to England.

\tLord Dalhousie (1848–1856) accelerated direct annexation through the Doctrine of Lapse. Under this rule, any protected state whose native ruler died without a natural male heir passed directly to the Company, refusing to recognize adopted sons as political successors. Dalhousie annexed Satara in 1848, Jaitpur and Sambalpur in 1849, Baghat in 1850, Udaipur in 1852, Jhansi in 1853, and Nagpur in 1854. In February 1856, Dalhousie annexed Awadh on grounds of persistent misgovernance based on James Outram's report, deposing Nawab Wajid Ali Shah. This provoked widespread outrage and directly fueled the discontent of Bengal Army sepoys.`,

7: `\tBritish land revenue policies replaced traditional agrarian systems to extract maximum surplus from the countryside. In Bengal, Bihar, and Orissa, Governor-General Lord Cornwallis introduced the Permanent Settlement (Kayamdhara Paddhat / Jameendari Paddhat) in 1793, planned by John Shore. Under this system, local zamindars became hereditary land owners. They had to pay a fixed revenue to the British, keeping 1/11th for themselves and handing over 10/11th to the Company. The Sunset Law (Suryast Kayda) mandated that if a zamindar failed to deposit the dues before sunset on the appointed date, his estate was confiscated and auctioned.

\tIn southern and southwestern India, including the Madras and Bombay Presidencies, Thomas Munro and Captain Alexander Read developed the Ryotwari System (Rayatwari Paddhat) between 1802 and 1820. Under this system, the British government bypassed zamindars and dealt directly with the ryots or cultivators (Rayat). Land was surveyed and revenue was fixed between 45% and 55% of the gross produce for periods of twenty to thirty years. Mountstuart Elphinstone extended this Ryotwari settlement across the newly annexed territories of Maharashtra following the Third Anglo-Maratha War. Heavy revenue demands forced cultivators to turn to local moneylenders (Savkar), causing widespread land alienation and rural indebtedness.

\tIn northwestern India, the Ganga Valley, Punjab, and parts of Central India, Holt Mackenzie conceived and Robert Merttins Bird implemented the Mahalwari System (Mahalwari Paddhat) in 1822 and 1833. Under Mahalwari, the unit of revenue assessment was the village or estate, called a mahal (Mahal). The village headman (Lambardar) or village community held joint responsibility for collecting and paying the revenue to the Company. These high cash extraction models destroyed the self-sufficient village economy (Gramin Swavalamban) and commercialized agriculture, forcing peasants to cultivate cash crops like indigo, opium, cotton, and jute instead of food grains.

\tIn administration, Lord Cornwallis organized the covenanted Civil Services, establishing the College of Fort William in Calcutta in 1800 to train young British officers. Lord Dalhousie utilized the Doctrine of Lapse (Dattak Varsa Namanjur Paddhat) to annex native states without natural heirs, denying the succession of adopted sons (Dattak Putra) in places like Satara, Jhansi, and Nagpur. Dalhousie also initiated the public works era: the first passenger railway ran from Bombay to Thane on April 16, 1853, the first electric telegraph line connected Calcutta to Agra in 1854, and the Post Office Act of 1854 established modern uniform postal rates across the country.`,

8: `\tBefore the great explosion of 1857, continuous armed resistance broke out across India against Company exploitation, forest laws, and land alienation. In Bengal, displaced peasants, demobilized soldiers, and wandering religious ascetics launched the Sanyasi and Fakir Rebellion (Sanyasi Fakir Band / Sanyasi Band) between 1763 and 1800, led by Majnu Shah, Chirag Ali, and Bhavani Pathak. Bankim Chandra Chatterjee later immortalized this resistance in his historical novel Anandamath, which contained the national song Vande Mataram. In western Maharashtra, the Koli and Bhil tribes resisted foreign rule; Ramoshi leaders like Umaji Naik raised the Ramoshi Rebellion (Ramoshi Band) in 1826 near Pune, issuing proclamations against British rule before his execution at Pune in 1832.

\tTribal uprisings occurred when the British opened isolated tribal homelands to outsiders, moneylenders, and contractors, collectively called Dikus (Bhadri Lok / Diku). In the Chota Nagpur plateau, the Kol Uprising (Kol Band) flared up in 1831–1832 under Buddho Bhagat and Madara Mahato against the transfer of ancestral tribal lands to Sikh and Muslim merchants. The Santhal Rebellion (Santhal Hul / Santhal Band) erupted in 1855–1856 across the Rajmahal Hills in the Damin-i-Koh region of modern Jharkhand. Under brothers Sidhu Murmu and Kanhu Murmu, along with Chand and Bhairav, over thirty thousand Santhals mobilized against oppressive moneylenders and British tax collectors. Armed with traditional bows and spears, the Santhals disrupted rail and postal links before the British declared martial law and suppressed them with cavalry. The Company subsequently created the separate Santhal Parganas district to conciliate the community.

\tFurther south in Orissa, the Paika Rebellion (Paika Vidroha / Paika Band) broke out in 1817. The Paikas were traditional landed peasant-militias serving the Raja of Khurda. When the British annexed Khurda in 1803, abolished the cowrie currency, and seized rent-free Paikan lands, Bakshi Jagabandhu Bidyadhara led an armed march on Puri. The Paikas defeated British detachments and burned government offices before the Company captured the fort and crushed the uprising through martial law. In coastal Maharashtra and Goa, the Sawantwadi Rebellion of the Phond Sawant and Subhedar lines challenged the British between 1838 and 1844.

\tReligious-political movements also mobilized resistance. The Wahabi Movement (Wahabi Chalval), founded by Syed Ahmed of Rae Bareilly, sought to overthrow British rule and replace it with Islamic governance, maintaining major underground networks between Sitana in the North-West Frontier and Patna in Bihar. In eastern Bengal, Haji Shariatullah founded the Faraizi Movement (Faraizi Chalval) in 1818, which was transformed into an agrarian peasant resistance against Hindu zamindars and British indigo planters by his son Dudu Miyan.`,

9: `\tThe suppression of the Revolt of 1857 (1857 cha Uthav) brought a permanent end to the hundred-year rule of the English East India Company in India. The British Parliament passed the Government of India Act on August 2, 1858 (1858 cha Sanadi Kayda / Bharat Sarkar Kayda 1858), transferring governance directly to the British Crown under Queen Victoria. The administrative authority previously shared by the Company's Court of Directors and the Board of Control was abolished and transferred to a new British Cabinet minister designated as the Secretary of State for India (Bharat Mantri), assisted by a fifteen-member advisory body called the Council of India (India Council) based in London.

\tIn India, the administrative head's title changed from Governor-General of India to Viceroy and Governor-General of India (Viceroy mhanje Rajpratinidhi), making Lord Canning the last Governor-General of the East India Company and the first Viceroy under the British Crown. On November 1, 1858, Lord Canning held a grand ceremonial court (Darbar) at Allahabad and read out the historic Queen's Proclamation (Rani cha Jahirnama). Often described as the Magna Carta of the Indian people, the proclamation promised that the British Crown would respect the territorial integrity of native states, abandon the aggressive policy of annexation, and officially abolish the Doctrine of Lapse (Dattak Varsa Namanjur Paddhat), permitting Indian princes to adopt heirs without British confiscation.

\tThe Queen's Proclamation also declared religious neutrality, promising that the colonial administration would not interfere with the religious beliefs or ancient customs of Indian subjects, and guaranteed equal eligibility for Indians to enter public civil services without discrimination based on race or creed. However, the British adopted a conscious policy of divide and rule (Foda ani Joda Niti) to prevent any future united resistance between Hindus and Muslims. Native princely states were treated as protective breakwaters against political unrest, preserving over five hundred local rulers under the supreme sovereignty or paramountcy of the British Crown.

\tTo prevent another armed mutiny among the armed forces, the British reorganized the military on the recommendations of the Peel Commission (Peel Aayog) of 1859. The proportion of British soldiers to Indian soldiers was dramatically increased: in the Bengal Army, the ratio was fixed at one European for every two Indian soldiers (1:2), while in the Madras and Bombay Presidencies, it was maintained at one to three (1:3). All heavy artillery and strategic weapons were reserved exclusively for European regiments. Recruitment was shifted away from the traditional high-caste regions of Awadh and Bihar toward groups designated as martial races (Ladhau Jati), such as Gurkhas, Sikhs, and Pathans, who had remained loyal to the British during the 1857 crisis.`,

10: `\tThe nineteenth century witnessed a major intellectual and cultural awakening termed the Indian Renaissance (Bharatiya Punarujjivan), aimed at purging social evils and reforming traditional customs. Raja Ram Mohan Roy, regarded as the Father of Modern Indian Renaissance (Adhunik Bharatache Janak), pioneered this phase. In 1814, he established the Atmiya Sabha in Calcutta to propagate monotheism and critique idolatry.

\tIn August 1828, he founded the Brahmo Sabha, later renamed the Brahmo Samaj (Brahmo Samaj), dedicated to the worship of one formless Supreme God, discarding rituals, caste hierarchy, and priesthood. Roy campaigned against the brutal custom of Sati; his relentless efforts led Governor-General Lord William Bentinck to pass the Sati Regulation Act of 1829 (Sati Bandi Kayda 1829), declaring Sati illegal and punishable as culpable homicide.

\tRoy also championed modern Western scientific education, assisting David Hare in establishing the Hindu College at Calcutta in 1817 and founding the Vedanta College in 1825. As a pioneer of nationalist journalism, he started the Bengali weekly Sambad Kaumudi in 1821 and the Persian newspaper Mirat-ul-Akbar in 1822 to spread progressive ideas.

\tThe Mughal Emperor Akbar II conferred upon him the title of Raja in 1830 and sent him as an envoy to England, where Roy died in Bristol in 1833. Following Roy's demise, Debendranath Tagore led the Brahmo Samaj, integrating his Tattvabodhini Sabha (founded in 1839) into the movement. In 1858, Keshab Chandra Sen joined the Samaj, expanding its reach outside Bengal to Bombay and Madras. Disagreements over radical social reforms caused a split in 1866, creating Debendranath's Adi Brahmo Samaj and Keshab's Brahmo Samaj of India.

\tIn northern India, Swami Dayananda Saraswati (Mool Shankar) launched a revivalist yet reformist current by establishing the Arya Samaj (Arya Samaj) at Bombay in April 1875, later shifting its headquarters to Lahore in 1877. Rejecting later ritualistic accretions, Dayananda raised the slogan "Go Back to the Vedas" (Vedankade Parata Chala) and wrote his core philosophical treatise Satyarth Prakash.

\tThe Arya Samaj opposed caste discrimination, child marriage, and idol worship, while defending the infallibility of the four original Vedas. Dayananda started the Shuddhi Movement (Shuddhi Chalval) to reconvert individuals who had converted to Islam or Christianity back to Hinduism, and organized Cow Protection Sabhas. Following his death in 1883, the Arya Samaj founded the Dayanand Anglo-Vedic (DAV) College Trust at Lahore in 1886, led by Lala Hansraj and Lala Lajpat Rai, while orthodox leaders like Swami Shraddhanand established the Gurukul Kangri at Haridwar in 1902 to promote traditional Vedic schooling.`,

11: `\tIn Maharashtra and Southern India, reform movements directly attacked caste hierarchy, untouchability, and gender oppression. In Pune, Mahatma Jyotirao Phule emerged as the pioneer of the anti-caste movement. In August 1848, Jyotirao and his wife Savitribai Phule opened India's first school for lower-caste girls at Bhide Wada in Pune.

\tOn September 24, 1873, Phule established the Satyashodhak Samaj (Truth-Seeking Society / Satyashodhak Samaj) to liberate Shudras and Ati-Shudras from Brahmanical dominance, religious superstitions, and economic exploitation. He authored seminal ideological texts, including Gulamgiri (Slavery) in 1873 dedicated to the abolition of black slavery in America and Shetkaryacha Asud (The Cultivator's Whip Cord) in 1881. In 1888, the public of Bombay conferred the title of "Mahatma" upon Phule. His close associate Narayan Meghaji Lokhande founded the Bombay Mill Hands Association in 1890, earning the title of the Father of the Indian Trade Union Movement.

\tAlongside non-Brahmin agitation, liberal Hindu reform took root in Bombay. In 1849, Dadoba Pandurang Tarkhadkar founded the Paramahansa Sabha to promote monotheism and abolition of caste barriers. Following its dissolution, Dr. Atmaram Pandurang, with the spiritual inspiration of Keshab Chandra Sen, founded the Prarthana Samaj (Prarthana Samaj) on March 31, 1867.

\tKey intellectuals like Justice Mahadev Govind Ranade and Sir Ramakrishna Gopal Bhandarkar transformed it into an influential social reform institution focusing on widow remarriage, female education, and inter-caste dining. In 1887, Ranade established the Indian National Social Conference (Bharatiya Samajik Parishad) to discuss progressive social legislation alongside sessions of the Indian National Congress. In 1906, Maharshi Vitthal Ramji Shinde established the Depressed Classes Mission of India (Asprushyata Nivaran Chalval) in Bombay to advance education and civil rights for untouchables.

\tIn the twentieth century, Dr. Bhimrao Ramji Ambedkar radicalized the Dalit liberation struggle. Ambedkar founded the Bahishkrit Hitakarini Sabha in Bombay in July 1924 with the motto "Educate, Agitate, and Organize" (Shikshan, Sangharsh, ani Sanghatan). On March 20, 1927, Ambedkar led the historic Mahad Satyagraha (Mahadcha Chavdar Tale Satyagraha) at the Chavdar Tank in Mahad to establish the fundamental civil right of untouchables to draw water from public water bodies.

\tOn December 25, 1927, Ambedkar publicly burned the ancient legal text Manusmriti (Manusmriti Dahan) at Mahad to symbolize the rejection of orthodox caste laws. In 1930, he launched the Kalaram Temple Satyagraha (Kalaram Mandir Satyagraha) in Nashik under Bhaurao Gaikwad. Ambedkar started influential newspapers including Mooknayak in 1920, Bahishkrit Bharat in 1927, and Janata in 1930, relentlessly demanding independent political representation for the depressed classes.`,

12: `\tNationalist political consciousness consolidated in the late nineteenth century through early regional political associations. In western India, Mahadev Govind Ranade, Ganesh Vasudev Joshi (Sarvajanik Kaka), and S. H. Chiplunkar founded the Poona Sarvajanik Sabha (Pune Sarvajanik Sabha) in April 1870 to bridge the gap between the British government and the public.

\tIn Bengal, Surendranath Banerjee and Ananda Mohan Bose established the Indian Association (Indian Association) in July 1876, organizing protests against the reduction of the maximum age limit for the Indian Civil Service examinations from twenty-one to nineteen years under Viceroy Lord Lytton. In the south, P. Anandacharlu, S. Ramaswami Mudaliar, and M. Veeraraghavachariar launched the Madras Mahajana Sabha in May 1884, while Pherozeshah Mehta, Kashinath Trimbak Telang, and Badruddin Tyabji formed the Bombay Presidency Association in January 1885.

\tThese regional associations coalesced into an all-India political platform through the initiative of Allan Octavian Hume, a retired British civil servant. The Indian National Congress (Rashtriya Sabha / Congress) held its inaugural session on December 28, 1885, at the Gokuldas Tejpal Sanskrit College in Bombay, after a cholera outbreak forced organizers to shift the venue from Pune.

\tThe first session was presided over by prominent Calcutta barrister Womesh Chandra Bonnerjee (W. C. Bonnerjee) and attended by seventy-two official delegates from across British India. Early British administrators, including Viceroy Lord Dufferin, initially tolerated the Congress under the so-called Safety Valve Theory (Safety Valve Siddhanta), believing an open forum would diffuse potential popular rebellions, but Dufferin soon derided the organization as representing a "microscopic minority" of Westernized elites.

\tThe early nationalist phase between 1885 and 1905 was dominated by Moderate leaders (Maval Gat / Maval Nete), including Dadabhai Naoroji, Gopal Krishna Gokhale, Pherozeshah Mehta, Dinshaw Wacha, and Surendranath Banerjee. The Moderates believed in British senses of justice and followed constitutional agitation using the method of the three P's: Prayer, Petition, and Protest (Arji, Vinanti, ani Nishedh). Their core demands included Indianization of the civil administration, simultaneous civil service examinations in England and India, expansion of legislative councils under the Indian Councils Act of 1892, reduction of military expenditures, and protection of domestic industries.

\tThe Moderates provided a pioneering economic critique of British imperialism. In his 1901 book Poverty and Un-British Rule in India, Dadabhai Naoroji formulated the Drain of Wealth Theory (Sampatti cha Apavahan Siddhanta / Sampatti Vahuni Jane), explaining how British rule extracted Indian capital through home charges, guaranteed railway profits, and unilateral trade surpluses without financial returns. In 1905, Gopal Krishna Gokhale established the Servants of India Society (Bharat Sevak Samaj) in Pune to train dedicated public workers for political and social development.`,

13: `\tViceroy Lord Curzon announced the Partition of Bengal (Vangal Bhang / Bengalchi Phani) on July 20, 1905, implementing it on October 16, 1905. The official British pretext claimed administrative convenience for governing an unwieldy province of seventy-eight million people. However, the real political motive was to divide the politically active Bengali intelligentsia along communal lines by carving out an Eastern Bengal and Assam province with a Muslim majority and leaving a western province with a Hindu majority.

\tThe day of partition was observed as a Day of Mourning (Shoka Din) across Bengal, marked by fasting, singing Rabindranath Tagore's patriotic song Amar Shonar Bangla, and tying Rakhis (Rakshabandhan) to symbolize undiminished national fraternity.

\tThe partition provoked the historic Swadeshi Movement (Swadeshi Chalval) and Boycott Movement (Bahishkar Chalval), formally declared at the Calcutta Town Hall on August 7, 1905. Leaders urged the public to discard British foreign goods and patronize indigenous industries, resulting in the establishment of local textile mills, national banks, and chemical factories such as Prafulla Chandra Ray's Bengal Chemicals.

\tIn education, Satish Chandra Mukherjee founded the Dawn Society, and the National Council of Education established the Bengal National College in August 1906, with Aurobindo Ghosh serving as its first principal. In Tamil Nadu, V. O. Chidambaram Pillai founded the Swadeshi Steam Navigation Company at Tuticorin to break the British shipping monopoly.

\tWithin the Congress, a ideological rift opened between the Moderates (Maval Gat) and the Extremists (Jahal Gat), led by the famous triumvirate of Lala Lajpat Rai in Punjab, Bal Gangadhar Tilak in Maharashtra, and Bipin Chandra Pal in Bengal, collectively known as Lal-Bal-Pal, alongside Aurobindo Ghosh. The Jahal leaders rejected petitions, advocating militant self-reliance, passive resistance, and complete Swaraj (Purna Swaraj).

\tTilak popularized nationalist mobilization through public Ganeshotsav and Shiv Jayanti festivals, declaring in his Marathi newspaper Kesari: "Swaraj is my birthright and I shall have it" (Swarajya ha maza janmasiddha hakka ahe ani to mi milavnarch). At the Calcutta session in December 1906, Dadabhai Naoroji managed to avert an open clash by declaring Swaraj as the ultimate goal of the Congress.

\tThe inevitable confrontation occurred at the Surat Congress session in December 1907 (Surat Phut) on the banks of the Tapti River, presided over by Rash Behari Ghosh. Heated disputes over resolutions on Swadeshi, Boycott, and National Education dissolved into physical disorder, forcing the Moderates to expel the Extremists from the Congress.

\tThe colonial government capitalized on the fracture by launching severe repression: Tilak was sentenced to six years of rigorous imprisonment in Mandalay Jail in Burma in 1908 for his articles in Kesari, while Lala Lajpat Rai was deported to Burma and Aurobindo Ghosh retired to Pondicherry to pursue spiritual philosophy.`,

14: `\tBritish authorities encouraged elite Muslim politicians to organize separately to counter Congress nationalism. On October 1, 1906, a delegation of thirty-five aristocratic Muslim leaders led by the Aga Khan met Viceroy Lord Minto at Shimla, an event known as the Shimla Deputation (Shimla Shishtamandal), petitioning for separate electorates and representation proportional to their historical and political status.

\tOn December 30, 1906, Nawab Salimullah of Dhaka convened the All India Muhammadan Educational Conference, where delegates resolved to establish the All India Muslim League (Muslim League) under the initial presidency of Nawab Viqar-ul-Mulk. The League's declared objectives were loyalty to the British Crown, protection of Muslim political rights, and countering the Congress's Boycott and Swadeshi campaigns.

\tTo win over Moderate leaders while institutionalizing communal divisions, the British Parliament enacted the Indian Councils Act of 1909, commonly known as the Morley-Minto Reforms (Morley-Minto Sudharana Kayda). Drafted by Secretary of State John Morley and Viceroy Lord Minto, the act expanded legislative councils at imperial and provincial levels.

\tCrucially, the 1909 act introduced separate electorates for Muslims (Vibhakt Matdarsangh), allowing Muslim voters to elect exclusively Muslim candidates. Lord Minto earned the designation of the Father of Communal Electorates (Sampradayik Matdarsanghache Janak). Satyendra Prasanna Sinha became the first Indian appointed to the Viceroy's Executive Council, serving as Law Member.

\tSimultaneously, young militants formed underground secret societies (Krantikari Gupta Sanghatana) to overthrow British rule through targeted assassinations, dacoities, and armed insurrections. In Maharashtra, Damodar and Balkrishna Chapekar (Chapekar Bandhu) assassinated the oppressive Plague Commissioner W. C. Rand and Lieutenant Ayerst at Pune on June 22, 1897.

\tIn 1899, Vinayak Damodar Savarkar and his brother Ganesh Savarkar founded Mitra Mela at Nashik, which evolved into the revolutionary society Abhinav Bharat (Abhinav Bharat) in 1904. On July 1, 1909, Madan Lal Dhingra assassinated British intelligence officer Curzon Wyllie at the Imperial Institute in London, while Anant Laxman Kanhere shot Nashik District Magistrate A. M. T. Jackson at the Vijayanand Theatre on December 21, 1909.

\tIn Bengal, Pramathanath Mitra, Jatindranath Banerjee, and Barindra Kumar Ghosh established the revolutionary Anushilan Samiti (Anushilan Samiti) in Calcutta in 1902. Barindra Ghosh and Bhupendranath Datta published the radical weekly Yugantar in 1906. On April 30, 1908, Khudiram Bose and Prafulla Chaki threw a bomb at a carriage in Muzaffarpur, attempting to assassinate the repressive Magistrate Douglas Kingsford, mistakenly killing the wife and daughter of Pringle Kennedy.

\tPrafulla Chaki shot himself to evade arrest, while eighteen-year-old Khudiram Bose was captured and hanged at Muzaffarpur jail on August 11, 1908. The subsequent Alipore Bomb Conspiracy Case (Alipore Bomb Khatla) led to the arrest of Aurobindo Ghosh, who was successfully defended in court by barrister Chittaranjan Das.`,

15: `\tThe outbreak of World War I in August 1914 intensified nationalist activities at home and abroad. Indian expatriates, primarily Punjabi Sikh immigrants, laborers, and ex-soldiers living in the United States and Canada, organized the Ghadar Party (Ghadar Paksha) in San Francisco in November 1913. Led by Lala Hardayal, Sohan Singh Bhakna, and Kartar Singh Sarabha, the party published a weekly revolutionary paper titled The Ghadar, carrying the banner "Angrezi Raj ka Dushman".

\tThe movement gained explosive momentum following the Komagata Maru Incident (Komagata Maru Ghatanavali) in May 1914, when Canadian authorities turned away a chartered Japanese steamship carrying 376 Indian passengers under Baba Gurdit Singh, leading to a deadly clash with British police at Budge Budge near Calcutta in September 1914. Ghadar activists returned to India to spark a military insurrection in the British Indian Army, but the plot was leaked, resulting in mass trials under the Lahore Conspiracy Case (Lahore Kat Khatla) and the execution of nineteen-year-old Kartar Singh Sarabha.

\tWithin India, nationalist leaders launched constitutional mass movements demanding self-government within the British Empire on the model of Irish Home Rule. Bal Gangadhar Tilak established his Indian Home Rule League (Home Rule League) at Belgaum in April 1916, covering Maharashtra (excluding Bombay city), the Central Provinces, Berar, and Karnataka, operating six branches through his journals Kesari and Mahratta.

\tIn September 1916, British theosophist Annie Besant founded the All India Home Rule League at Madras, covering the rest of India with two hundred branches, supported by George Arundale, B. P. Wadia, and C. P. Ramaswamy Aiyar, utilizing her newspapers New India and Commonweal. Prominent leaders like Jawaharlal Nehru and Muhammad Ali Jinnah joined Besant's league. Tilak and Besant conducted extensive lecture tours, building massive political consciousness and pressing the British for constitutional autonomy.

\tThe historic annual session of the Indian National Congress met at Lucknow in December 1916 under the presidency of Ambica Charan Mazumdar. Two decisive political reconciliations took place at this meeting. First, through the patient mediation of Annie Besant and Tilak, the Moderate faction (Maval Gat) and the Extremist faction (Jahal Gat) reunited nine years after the bitter Surat Split of 1907.

\tSecond, the Congress and the All India Muslim League concluded the historic Lucknow Pact (Lucknow Karar) on December 29, 1916, largely negotiated by Bal Gangadhar Tilak and Muhammad Ali Jinnah, who was hailed by Sarojini Naidu as the "Ambassador of Hindu-Muslim Unity". Under the pact, the Congress conceded the League's demand for separate communal electorates (Vibhakt Matdarsangh) in exchange for joint political pressure on the British government to demand provincial autonomy and Dominion Status.`,

16: `\tMohandas Karamchand Gandhi returned to India from South Africa on January 9, 1915, a date now observed as Pravasi Bharatiya Divas. In South Africa, Gandhi had developed and tested his signature methods of non-violent resistance: Satyagraha (Satya mhanje khare ani agraha mhanje dharun thevane / Satyagraha) and Ahimsa (Ahimsa).

\tUpon his return, he accepted Gopal Krishna Gokhale as his political mentor (Rajakiya Guru). On Gokhale's advice, Gandhi spent an entire year traveling across India by third-class railway carriages to understand grassroots conditions before participating in active politics, setting up the Sabarmati Ashram at Ahmedabad in May 1915.

\tGandhi entered active political agitation in India through three localized regional struggles. The first was the Champaran Satyagraha (Champaran Satyagraha) in Bihar in April 1917, which served as his First Civil Disobedience Movement (Pahili Savinay Kaydebhang Chalval). European planters compelled local peasants to cultivate indigo on 3/20th of their total landholdings under the oppressive Tinkathia system (Tinkathia Paddhat).

\tAt the Lucknow Congress session in 1916, a local farmer named Raj Kumar Shukla persuaded Gandhi to visit Champaran. Despite an official order to leave the district, Gandhi refused and faced trial with prominent associates Rajendra Prasad, J. B. Kripalani, and Mahadev Desai. The government formed an inquiry commission with Gandhi as a member, leading to the abolition of the Tinkathia system and the refund of 25% of illegally extracted money to the peasants. Rabindranath Tagore conferred the title "Mahatma" upon Gandhi during this agitation.

\tIn February 1918, Gandhi intervened in the Ahmedabad Mill Strike (Ahmedabad Girni Kamgar Samp) in Gujarat, marking his First Hunger Strike (Pahile Uposhan). The conflict arose between local textile mill owners and mill hands over the discontinuation of the Plague Bonus (Plague Bonus).

\tWhile workers demanded a 50% wage hike to cope with wartime inflation, mill owners offered only 20%. Gandhi supported the workers, led by Anasuya Sarabhai, and undertook a fast unto death. The arbitration committee yielded, granting workers a 35% wage increase.

\tIn March 1918, Gandhi led the Kheda Satyagraha (Kheda Satyagraha) in Gujarat, his First Non-Cooperation movement (Pahili Asahakar Chalval). Due to severe crop failure, the agrarian harvest fell below one-fourth of normal yield. Under the revenue code, peasants were legally entitled to full tax remission, but British officials demanded coercive tax collection. Supported by Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel and Indulal Yagnik, Gandhi organized peasants to withhold land revenue. The British authorities surrendered and issued secret orders to collect revenue only from those peasants capable of paying.`,

17: `\tIn March 1919, the Imperial Legislative Council passed the Anarchical and Revolutionary Crimes Act, universally known as the Rowlatt Act (Rowlatt Kayda), based on the recommendations of the Sedition Committee chaired by Sir Sidney Rowlatt. This repressive legislation authorized the colonial government to detain any Indian suspected of subversive activities for up to two years without trial, denying the basic legal rights of legal representation, appeal, or evidence examination, summarized in the popular protest slogan: "No Dalil, No Vakil, No Appeal" (Dalil Nahi, Vakil Nahi, Appeal Nahi). Gandhi organized the Rowlatt Satyagraha Sabha and declared a nationwide general strike (Hartal) on April 6, 1919, marking his first nationwide political mobilization.

\tTensions erupted violently in Punjab following the arrest and deportation of two popular nationalist leaders, Dr. Saifuddin Kitchlew and Dr. Satyapal, on April 9, 1919, by the Lieutenant-Governor of Punjab, Sir Michael O'Dwyer. On April 13, 1919, the day of the Baisakhi festival, a peaceful crowd of ten to twenty thousand men, women, and children gathered inside the walled garden of Jallianwala Bagh in Amritsar to protest these arrests.

\tBrigadier-General Reginald Dyer marched in with Gurkha and Baluch riflemen, blocked the sole narrow exit, and ordered his troops to open fire without issuing any dispersal warning. The firing continued for ten minutes until ammunition was exhausted, expending 1,650 rounds and slaughtering hundreds of civilians, an atrocity known as the Jallianwala Bagh Massacre (Jallianwala Bagh Hatyakand).

\tIn moral revulsion against the slaughter, Rabindranath Tagore renounced his British Knighthood (Sir Padvi), and Mahatma Gandhi surrendered his Boer War medal Kaiser-i-Hind (Kaiser-i-Hind Padvi). Under intense public pressure, the British government appointed the Disorders Inquiry Committee under Lord William Hunter, known as the Hunter Commission (Hunter Aayog), which censured Dyer for an error of judgment but imposed no penal prosecution. Simultaneously, the Indian National Congress appointed an independent inquiry committee comprising Gandhi, Motilal Nehru, and C. R. Das to document the brutality.

\tSimultaneously, Indian Muslims launched the Khilafat Movement (Khilafat Chalval) under the leadership of the Ali brothers, Maulana Mohammad Ali and Shaukat Ali, along with Maulana Abul Kalam Azad. The movement aimed to defend the temporal and spiritual authority of the Ottoman Sultan, who was revered as the Caliph of Islam (Khalifa), following the dismemberment of the Ottoman Empire by the Allied powers through the Treaty of Sevres in 1920.

\tGandhi recognized this pan-Islamic agitation as a unique historical opportunity to forge enduring Hindu-Muslim unity (Hindu-Muslim Ekya), securing the presidency of the All India Khilafat Conference in Delhi in November 1919 and convincing the Congress to link the Khilafat grievances directly with the demand for Swaraj.`,

18: `\tIn September 1920, the Indian National Congress held a special session at Calcutta under the presidency of Lala Lajpat Rai to approve Mahatma Gandhi's proposal for non-cooperation with the British government. The regular annual session met at Nagpur in December 1920 under C. Vijayaraghavachariar, formally endorsing the historic Non-Cooperation Movement (Asahakar Chalval) resolution and redefining the Congress goal as the attainment of Swaraj through peaceful and legitimate means. The Nagpur session reorganized the Congress along linguistic lines, established village-level Congress committees, and created a fifteen-member Congress Working Committee (Congress Karyakarini) to direct nationwide political agitation.

\tThe operational program of the movement had two distinct aspects: destructive boycott and constructive work. The boycott involved surrendering government titles and honorary offices, boycotting civil services, courts, government schools, colleges, and upcoming provincial council elections under the Government of India Act of 1919 (Montagu-Chelmsford Reforms / Montagu-Chelmsford Sudharana Kayda). Lawyers like Motilal Nehru, Chittaranjan Das, C. Rajagopalachari, and Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel gave up lucrative legal practices. Thousands of students abandoned official educational institutions, leading to the creation of national universities such as Jamia Millia Islamia, Kashi Vidyapith, Gujarat Vidyapith, and Bihar Vidyapith. The constructive program emphasized mass spinning of Khadi on the charkha (Charkha ani Khadi), setting up arbitration panchayats (Panchayat Nyayalaya), establishing national schools, and organizing the Tilak Swaraj Fund (Tilak Swarajya Fund), which collected over one crore rupees within months to commemorate Lokmanya Tilak, who died on August 1, 1920.

\tThe movement generated unprecedented mass mobilization across urban and rural India, but sudden violence cut it short. On February 4, 1922, a procession of two thousand peasant protesters clashed with local police at Chauri Chaura (Chauri Chaura Ghatanavali) in the Gorakhpur district of the United Provinces. The police opened fire on the crowd; enraged protesters retaliated by locking twenty-two policemen inside the local police post (Thane) and setting the building on fire, incinerating all inside.

\tStunned by this deviation from non-violence, Gandhi declared that the people were not yet spiritually prepared for Satyagraha, terming his premature launch a "Himalayan blunder" (Himalayaevadhi Chook). Overriding the strong objections of younger leaders like Subhash Chandra Bose and Jawaharlal Nehru, Gandhi convened the Congress Working Committee at Bardoli on February 12, 1922, and passed the Bardoli Resolution (Bardoli Tharao), immediately suspending the Non-Cooperation Movement. The British government capitalized on the resulting demoralization: on March 10, 1922, Viceroy Lord Reading ordered the arrest of Gandhi, who was tried by Judge C. N. Broomfield at Ahmedabad and sentenced to six years of simple imprisonment for sedition.`,

19: `\tThe suspension of the Non-Cooperation Movement created widespread frustration and a sharp political divide within the Congress. At the Gaya Congress session in December 1922, chaired by Chittaranjan Das, leaders split into two factions: the Pro-Changers (Pherwadi) and the No-Changers (Napherwadi). The Pro-Changers, led by C. R. Das, Motilal Nehru, and N. C. Kelkar, advocated entering the central and provincial legislative councils under the 1919 Act to obstruct colonial legislation from within, famously describing their tactic as "wrecking the councils from inside".

\tThe No-Changers, including Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel, Rajendra Prasad, C. Rajagopalachari, and M. A. Ansari, opposed council entry and insisted on adhering strictly to Gandhi's constructive program of village reconstruction, Khadi spinning, and anti-untouchability work.

\tOn January 1, 1923, C. R. Das and Motilal Nehru formed the Congress-Khilafat Swarajya Party, commonly known as the Swaraj Party (Swarajya Paksha), with Das as president and Nehru as secretary. In the November 1923 elections, the Swarajists captured forty-two out of one hundred and one elected seats in the Central Legislative Assembly and won an absolute majority in the Central Provinces.

\tIn 1925, they achieved a major constitutional triumph when prominent Swarajist leader Vithalbhai Patel was elected the first Indian President (Speaker) of the Central Legislative Assembly (Kendriya Kaydemandalache Adhyaksh). Following the sudden death of C. R. Das in June 1925, internal dissensions weakened the party, causing a faction led by M. R. Jayakar and N. C. Kelkar to break away as Responsivists (Pratisaadwadi) to cooperate with the government.

\tSimultaneously, young militants disillusioned by Gandhi's sudden withdrawal turned back to revolutionary armed struggle. In October 1924, Ram Prasad Bismil, Sachindra Nath Sanyal, Ashfaqulla Khan, and Jogesh Chandra Chatterjee met at Kanpur and founded the Hindustan Republican Association (HRA / Hindustan Republican Association). Sanyal published the ideological manifesto The Revolutionary.

\tTo finance arms acquisitions from abroad, ten HRA revolutionaries held up the 8-Down passenger train near Lucknow on August 9, 1925, seizing the British government treasury in the famous Kakori Train Action (Kakori Train Kat). The colonial government prosecuted twenty-nine revolutionaries under the Kakori Conspiracy Case; Ram Prasad Bismil, Ashfaqulla Khan, Roshan Singh, and Rajendra Lahiri were hanged in December 1927, while Sachindra Nath Sanyal was sentenced to life imprisonment in the Andaman Cellular Jail.

\tIn September 1928, the surviving revolutionaries led by Chandrashekhar Azad gathered at Feroz Shah Kotla ground in Delhi. Joined by Bhagat Singh, Sukhdev Thapar, and Shivaram Rajguru of the Naujawan Bharat Sabha, they adopted socialism as their ideological goal and renamed the organization the Hindustan Socialist Republican Association (HSRA / Hindustan Socialist Republican Association), committing to the overthrow of both foreign imperialism and domestic capitalist exploitation through popular armed insurrection.`,

20: `\tIn November 1927, the British government appointed the Indian Statutory Commission, popularly known as the Simon Commission (Simon Aayog), two years ahead of schedule to review the constitutional working of the Government of India Act of 1919. Chaired by Sir John Simon, the seven-member parliamentary panel contained not a single Indian representative, provoking unanimous national condemnation as an "All-White Commission" (Sampurna Gora Aayog).

\tWhen the commission landed in Bombay on February 3, 1928, it was greeted with black flags, nationwide general strikes (Hartal), and the resounding slogan: "Simon Go Back" (Simon Parat Ja). During a massive anti-Simon demonstration at Lahore on October 30, 1928, British police superintendent James A. Scott ordered a brutal lathi charge; Assistant Superintendent J. P. Saunders assaulted sixty-three-year-old Lala Lajpat Rai, who succumbed to severe chest injuries on November 17, 1928, after prophetically declaring that every blow struck on him would prove a nail in the coffin of the British Empire.

\tTo avenge Rai's death, HSRA revolutionaries Bhagat Singh, Shivaram Rajguru, and Sukhdev Thapar, supported by Chandrashekhar Azad, assassinated Saunders outside the police headquarters in Lahore on December 17, 1928. Months later, on April 8, 1929, Bhagat Singh and Batukeshwar Dutt threw two harmless smoke bombs and leaflets into the Central Legislative Assembly in Delhi to protest the repressive Public Safety Bill and Trade Disputes Bill, courting arrest while shouting "Inquilab Zindabad" (Long Live Revolution / Kranti Chiranjeev Ho).

\tMeanwhile, Birkenhead, the Secretary of State for India, insolently challenged Indian leaders to draft an acceptable constitutional scheme. An All-Parties Conference appointed a committee chaired by Motilal Nehru, which submitted the Nehru Report (Nehru Ahaval) in August 1928. The report demanded Dominion Status (Vashahaticha Swarajya), nineteen fundamental rights, universal adult suffrage, and linguistic provinces, rejecting separate communal electorates in favor of joint electorates with minority reservations. Muhammad Ali Jinnah rejected the Nehru Report and formulated his Fourteen Points (Chowda Mudde) in March 1929, demanding rigid minority quotas.

\tYounger radicals within the Congress, led by Subhash Chandra Bose and Jawaharlal Nehru, rejected Dominion Status and demanded complete severance from the British Crown. The historic annual session of the Congress convened at Lahore on the banks of the Ravi River in December 1929, presided over by Jawaharlal Nehru.

\tThe assembly adopted the momentous Purna Swaraj resolution (Purna Swarajya Tharao), declaring complete independence as the sole political objective of the national struggle. At midnight on December 31, 1929, Nehru unfurled the tricolor national flag. The Congress Working Committee declared January 26, 1930, as the first Independence Day (Purna Swarajya Din), asking the public across India to take a solemn national pledge of total non-cooperation with British imperial rule.`,

21: `\tOn January 31, 1930, Mahatma Gandhi presented his famous Eleven-Point Ultimatum (Akra Muddyanchi Magani) to Viceroy Lord Irwin, demanding the abolition of the salt tax, reduction of land revenue by fifty percent, release of political prisoners, and reduction of military expenditure. When Irwin ignored the letter, Gandhi launched the Civil Disobedience Movement (Savinay Kaydebhang Chalval).

\tOn March 12, 1930, Gandhi began the historic Salt March (Dandi Yatra / Mithacha Satyagraha) from Sabarmati Ashram with seventy-eight chosen followers. Walking 240 miles through Gujarat over twenty-four days, Gandhi reached the coastal village of Dandi on April 5, 1930. On the morning of April 6, 1930, he picked up a lump of natural salt from the seashore, technically violating the British salt monopoly and inaugurating nationwide civil disobedience.

\tThe movement ignited mass salt satyagrahas along India's coastline. In Tamil Nadu, C. Rajagopalachari led a march from Tiruchirappalli to Vedaranyam (Vedaranyam Yatra). In Malabar, K. Kelappan marched from Calicut to Payyanur. In the North-West Frontier Province, Khan Abdul Ghaffar Khan (Badshah Khan / Sarhad Gandhi) mobilized his non-violent volunteer brigade, the Khudai Khidmatgar (Devache Sevak) or Red Shirts (Lal Dagli), launching massive protests in Peshawar.

\tIn western India, Sarojini Naidu, Imam Saheb, and Gandhi's son Manilal led twenty-five hundred peaceful satyagrahis in an assault on the Dharasana Salt Works (Dharasana Satyagraha) on May 21, 1930, facing brutal lathi charges documented by American journalist Webb Miller. In Maharashtra, forest law defiance erupted as Forest Satyagrahas (Jungle Satyagraha) in places like Bilashi, Sangamner, and Chirner.

\tTo discuss constitutional reforms, the British government convened three Round Table Conferences (Golmej Parishad) in London. The First Round Table Conference (Pahili Golmej Parishad) met in November 1930 under British Prime Minister Ramsay MacDonald; the Congress boycotted it completely, rendering negotiations fruitless. Realizing the futility of talks without the Congress,

\tLord Irwin released Gandhi, culminating in the Gandhi-Irwin Pact (Gandhi-Irwin Karar / Delhi Karar) on March 5, 1931. Under this agreement, the Congress suspended civil disobedience and agreed to participate in the second conference, while the British released non-violent political prisoners and allowed coastal salt manufacturing.

\tMahatma Gandhi sailed to London aboard the SS Rajputana as the sole representative of the Congress to attend the Second Round Table Conference (Dusri Golmej Parishad) in September 1931. The conference deadlocked over the demand for separate communal electorates raised by minority representatives, including B. R. Ambedkar for the Depressed Classes and Muhammad Ali Jinnah for Muslims. Disappointed, Gandhi returned empty-handed to India in December 1931. When Viceroy Lord Willingdon resumed severe police repression, Gandhi restarted the movement, leading to his arrest and imprisonment in Yerwada Central Jail in Pune.`,
  
22: `\tFollowing the collapse of the Second Round Table Conference, British Prime Minister Ramsay MacDonald announced the Communal Award ( जातीय निवाडा / Jatiya Nivada ) on August 16, 1932. The award accorded separate electorates (Vibhakt Matdarsangh) not only to Muslims, Sikhs, and Europeans, but also classified the Depressed Classes (Dalits) as a distinct minority community entitled to separate representation.

\tMahatma Gandhi, imprisoned in Yerwada Central Jail in Pune, viewed this move as an attempt to permanently divide Hindu society and weaken the national freedom movement. On September 20, 1932, Gandhi launched a fast unto death (Amarn Uposhan) inside Yerwada Jail to protest against separate electorates for Dalits, insisting instead on joint electorates with reserved legislative seats.

\tIntense negotiations between caste Hindu leaders including Madan Mohan Malaviya, C. Rajagopalachari, and M. R. Jayakar and Dalit representative Dr. B. R. Ambedkar produced the historic Poona Pact (Pune Karar) on September 24, 1932. Dr. Ambedkar agreed to surrender separate electorates in exchange for substantially increased political representation for the Depressed Classes.

\tUnder the pact, seats reserved for Dalits in provincial legislatures were increased from seventy-one (granted by the Communal Award) to one hundred and forty-eight under joint electorates (Samayik Matdarsangh), alongside eighteen percent reservation in the Central Legislative Assembly. Gandhi broke his fast, and the British government amended the Communal Award to incorporate the agreement. The Third Round Table Conference (Tisri Golmej Parishad) met in London in late 1932 with meager attendance; the Congress, British Labour Party, and major princes boycotted the proceedings entirely.

\tThe discussions culminated in the Government of India Act of 1935 (Bharat Sarkar Kayda 1935), enacted in August 1935. The act proposed an All-India Federation (Akhil Bharatiya Sangharajya) comprising British Indian provinces and princely states, which never materialized because princely rulers refused to surrender sovereignty. Crucially, the 1935 act abolished Dyarchy (Dwikendri Paddhat) in the provinces and established complete Provincial Autonomy (Prantik Swattata).

\tIn place of provincial dyarchy, the act introduced dyarchy at the federal center, dividing federal subjects into Reserved (Rakheev) and Transferred (Sopiv) portfolios. It established a Federal Court (Sangharajya Nyayalaya) in Delhi on October 1, 1937, which later became the Supreme Court of India on January 28, 1950, set up the Reserve Bank of India (RBI), and separated Burma from British India.

\tIn early 1937, elections were held for eleven provincial assemblies under the new act. The Congress contested the elections, sweeping absolute majorities in Madras, the United Provinces, Central Provinces, Bihar, and Orissa, while forming coalition governments in Bombay, Assam, and the North-West Frontier Province under provincial prime ministers (Prantik Mukhyamantri).`,

23: `\tThe outbreak of World War II on September 1, 1939, when Nazi Germany invaded Poland, created an acute constitutional crisis in India. On September 3, Viceroy Lord Linlithgow declared India a belligerent on the side of Great Britain without consulting Indian political leaders or elected provincial ministries. Protesting this unilateral imperial dragnet, the Congress Working Committee demanded an immediate declaration of British war aims regarding Indian independence.

\tWhen the British government gave evasive responses, the Congress high command ordered all eight Congress provincial ministries to resign simultaneously in late October and November 1939. Muhammad Ali Jinnah and the Muslim League celebrated this mass resignation as a Day of Deliverance (Mukti Din) on December 22, 1939. Months later, at its Lahore session in March 1940, the Muslim League formally passed the historic Pakistan Resolution (Lahore Tharao / Pakistan Tharao), demanding sovereign autonomous Muslim homelands.

\tSeeking Indian cooperation in the war effort after the fall of France, Viceroy Linlithgow announced the August Offer (August Prastav) on August 8, 1940. It offered the expansion of the Governor-General's Executive Council, the inclusion of more Indians, and the post-war drafting of a constitution by an Indian body, while insisting on minority veto power and granting only Dominion Status (Vashahaticha Swarajya). Both the Congress and the Muslim League rejected the proposal outright.

\tIn response, Mahatma Gandhi launched the Individual Satyagraha (Vaidyaktik Satyagraha) in October 1940 to affirm the democratic right to free speech against the war without provoking mass unrest. Acharya Vinoba Bhave was selected as the first Satyagrahi (Pahile Satyagrahi), followed by Jawaharlal Nehru as the second. Over twenty-five thousand individual satyagrahis courted arrest shouting anti-war slogans, an agitation termed the Delhi Chalo Movement (Delhi Chalo Chalval).

\tBy early 1942, rapid Japanese military advances across Southeast Asia capturing Rangoon, Singapore, and Burma—brought the Pacific War directly to the borders of eastern India. Under intense diplomatic pressure from American President Franklin D. Roosevelt and Chinese leader Chiang Kai-shek, British Prime Minister Winston Churchill dispatched a mission to Delhi in March 1942 headed by Sir Stafford Cripps, known as the Cripps Mission (Cripps Yojna). Cripps proposed Dominion Status after the end of hostilities, the creation of a Constituent Assembly elected by provincial legislatures and nominated by princes, and gave provinces the right to secede and form separate constitutions.

\tThe Congress rejected the proposals because they lacked immediate executive power-sharing and contained seeds of partition, while the League rejected them for not directly creating Pakistan. Gandhi famously dismissed the Cripps proposals as a "post-dated cheque on a crashing bank" (Budiankya Bankecha Mudati Nantarcha Cheque).`,

24: `\tFollowing the failure of the Cripps Mission, Mahatma Gandhi decided that an immediate British exit was imperative to protect India from Japanese invasion. On July 14, 1942, the Congress Working Committee met at Wardha and passed the historic Quit India resolution (Chale Jav Tharao / Bharat Chhodo Tharao).

\tThe All India Congress Committee met at the Gowalia Tank Maidan in Bombay (later named August Kranti Maidan) on August 8, 1942, under the presidency of Maulana Abul Kalam Azad, ratifying the movement. Gandhi delivered his famous fiery address, giving the nation the historic mantra: "Do or Die" (Karenge Ya Marenge / Karu Kivha Maru), calling upon every citizen to act as a free individual and demand complete, immediate British departure from Indian soil.

\tIn the early hours of August 9, 1942, the colonial government struck back under Operation Zero Hour (Operation Zero Hour), arresting the entire top nationalist leadership. Gandhi was imprisoned in the Aga Khan Palace in Pune, while Jawaharlal Nehru, Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel, and Abul Kalam Azad were detained in Ahmednagar Fort.

\tDeprived of central leadership, the agitation transformed into a spontaneous, decentralized mass uprising known as the August Kranti (August Kranti). Angry crowds attacked government buildings, burned police outposts, derailed freight trains, and cut telegraph wires across Bombay, Bihar, the United Provinces, and Bengal.

\tA younger generation of underground socialist revolutionaries led the resistance. Jayaprakash Narayan, Ram Manohar Lohia, and Aruna Asaf Ali, who hoisted the national flag at Gowalia Tank on August 9, operated underground operational networks. In Bombay, Usha Mehta and Vithaldas Jhaveri operated an illegal underground radio station, the Congress Radio (Congress Radio), broadcasting uncensored nationalist news across the country until British police seized the transmitter in November 1942.

\tA defining feature of the 1942 rebellion was the establishment of parallel governments (Pratisarkar) that replaced British authority in liberated pockets. In Ballia in eastern Uttar Pradesh, Chittu Pandey established the first parallel government in August 1942, releasing prisoners and taking over civil administration. In Tamluk in the Midnapore district of Bengal, rebels organized the Tamluk Jatiya Sarkar (Tamluk Jatiya Sarkar), which functioned from December 1942 to September 1944 and commanded an armed volunteer corps named Vidyut Vahini; seventy-three-year-old peasant woman Matangini Hazra was shot dead while holding the national flag.

\tIn Maharashtra, Nana Patil (Krantisinha Nana Patil) established the Pratisarkar (Patri Sarkar) in Satara in 1943. Supported by Y. B. Chavan, Kisan Veer, and Nagnath Nayakawadi, the Satara Pratisarkar set up people's courts (Nyayadan Mandale), punished informants and corrupt moneylenders, and effectively suspended colonial revenue administration across hundreds of villages until 1946.`,

25: `\tSubhash Chandra Bose, elected Congress President at Haripura in 1938 and re-elected at Tripuri in 1939 after defeating Gandhi's candidate Pattabhi Sitaramayya, resigned following ideological friction to form the Forward Bloc (Forward Bloc) within the Congress in May 1939. Placed under house arrest in Calcutta, Bose escaped dramatically in January 1941 disguised as Ziauddin.

\tHe traveled across Peshawar, Kabul, and Moscow to Berlin, establishing the Free India Centre and Azad Hind Radio with German backing. In Southeast Asia, Indian nationalist revolutionary Rash Behari Bose had organized the Indian Independence League, while Captain Mohan Singh of the British Indian Army formed the first division of the Indian National Army (Azad Hind Fauj / INA) in Singapore in 1942 from captured Indian prisoners of war.

\tIn July 1943, Subhash Chandra Bose arrived in Singapore aboard a submarine, assuming supreme command of the Azad Hind Fauj. Hailed as Netaji, he coined inspiring national slogans: "Give me blood, and I shall give you freedom" (Tum Mujhe Khoon Do, Main Tumhe Azadi Doonga) and "Delhi Chalo" (Delhi Chalo). On October 21, 1943, Netaji proclaimed the Provisional Government of Free India (Azad Hind Sarkar) in Singapore, recognized by Axis powers. Japan transferred the Andaman and Nicobar Islands to the provisional government, which Bose renamed Shahid Dweep and Swaraj Dweep (Shahid ani Swarajya Dweep).

\tThe INA organized four regiments: Subhash, Gandhi, Nehru, and an all-female combat unit named the Rani of Jhansi Regiment (Jhansi Rani Regiment) commanded by Captain Lakshmi Sahgal. In early 1944, the INA advanced through Burma alongside Japanese forces, capturing Kohima and planting the tricolor on Indian soil at Moirang in Manipur, before severe monsoon rains, logistical collapse, and Japanese retreats forced a general withdrawal. Netaji reportedly died following an aircraft crash at Taihoku in Taiwan on August 18, 1945.

\tThe British government decided to hold public military court-martials of captured INA officers inside the Red Fort in Delhi in late 1945, known as the Red Fort Trials (Lal Killa Khatla / INA Khatla). Three key officers Colonel Prem Kumar Sahgal, Colonel Gurbaksh Singh Dhillon, and Major Shah Nawaz Khan representing Hindu, Sikh, and Muslim communities, were jointly tried for treason. A distinguished legal defense committee headed by Bhulabhai Desai, Tej Bahadur Sapru, and Jawaharlal Nehru defended them amidst massive nationwide street demonstrations, forcing Commander-in-Chief Claude Auchinleck to commute their life sentences.

\tThe culminating blow to imperial control occurred on February 18, 1946, with the Royal Indian Navy Mutiny (Navsena Vidroha / Talwar Navik Uthav). Ratings (naval sailors) aboard the signal-training ship HMIS Talwar in Bombay went on strike under B. C. Dutt, protesting racial discrimination, unpalatable food, and the arrest of colleagues. The revolt quickly spread to seventy-eight ships, twenty shore establishments, and twenty thousand sailors across Bombay, Karachi, and Calcutta.

\tRatings hoisted Congress, Muslim League, and Communist flags on ship masts. Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel and M. A. Jinnah intervened to convince the naval ratings to surrender on February 23, 1946, but the rebellion demonstrated that the British could no longer rely on Indian armed forces to sustain colonial rule.`,

26: `\tIn June 1945, Viceroy Lord Wavell convened a conference of twenty-one political leaders at Shimla, known as the Shimla Conference (Shimla Parishad), to negotiate the Wavell Plan (Wavell Yojna). The proposal sought to reconstitute the Viceroy's Executive Council with entirely Indian members, excluding the Viceroy and the Commander-in-Chief, offering equal representation to Caste Hindus and Muslims.

\tThe conference collapsed when Muhammad Ali Jinnah demanded that the All India Muslim League held the exclusive monopoly to nominate all Muslim members, a claim rejected by the Congress, whose delegation was led by its Muslim president, Maulana Abul Kalam Azad. In the winter of 1945–1946, general elections took place across British India; the Congress captured the majority of general seats, while the Muslim League swept nearly all reserved Muslim seats, solidifying political polarization.

\tTo resolve the constitutional deadlock and negotiate the transfer of power, British Prime Minister Clement Attlee dispatched a high-powered three-member Cabinet Mission (Cabinet Mission / Mantrimandal Shishtamandal) to India on March 24, 1946. The delegation comprised Lord Pethick-Lawrence (Secretary of State for India), Sir Stafford Cripps (President of the Board of Trade), and A. V. Alexander (First Lord of the Admiralty).

\tThe Cabinet Mission rejected the full demand for an independent sovereign Pakistan as economically and militarily unviable. Instead, it proposed an undivided three-tier Indian Federation with a weak center controlling defense, foreign affairs, and communications, while granting all residual powers to the provinces.

\tThe plan organized provinces into three autonomous groups: Section A (Hindu-majority provinces including Madras, Bombay, United Provinces, Bihar, Central Provinces, and Orissa), Section B (northwestern Muslim-majority provinces including Punjab, Sindh, and North-West Frontier Province), and Section C (northeastern provinces including Bengal and Assam).

\tIt also provided for the formation of an interim national government and the election of a Constituent Assembly (Ghatanaparishad) by provincial legislatures through proportional representation. Although both the Congress and the Muslim League initially accepted the long-term plan, Jawaharlal Nehru stated at a press conference in Bombay in July 1946 that the Congress entered the Constituent Assembly unfettered by rules, implying that provincial grouping could be rejected.

\tReacting sharply to Nehru's statement, the Muslim League withdrew its acceptance on July 29, 1946. Jinnah rejected constitutional methods and called for Direct Action Day (Pratyaksha Kruti Din) on August 16, 1946, to force the creation of Pakistan. Widespread communal riots, known as the Great Calcutta Killings (Calcutta Hatyakand), erupted under Bengal Premier H. S. Suhrawardy, resulting in over four thousand deaths in four days and spreading horrific communal violence to Noakhali, Bihar, and western Punjab. Mahatma Gandhi camped in Noakhali, walking barefoot between riot-torn villages to restore communal peace.`,

27: `\tViceroy Wavell invited Jawaharlal Nehru to form the Interim Government (Antarim Sarkar), which assumed office on September 2, 1946, with Nehru as Vice-President of the Executive Council. The Muslim League boycotted initially but joined on October 26, 1946, to obstruct administration from within; Liaquat Ali Khan became Finance Member and systematically stalled council operations.

\tMeanwhile, the Constituent Assembly convened for its historic first session in New Delhi on December 9, 1946. Dr. Sachchidananda Sinha served as interim President until Dr. Rajendra Prasad was elected permanent President on December 11, with Sir B. N. Rau appointed Constitutional Adviser. On December 13, 1946, Nehru introduced the historic Objectives Resolution (Uddishta Tharao), outlining the founding democratic principles of the sovereign Indian republic, while the Muslim League boycotted all assembly proceedings.

\tOn February 20, 1947, British Prime Minister Clement Attlee delivered an epochal statement in the House of Commons, declaring Britain's definite intention to transfer full power to responsible Indian hands no later than June 30, 1948. Attlee also announced the recall of Lord Wavell and the appointment of Rear-Admiral Lord Louis Mountbatten as the thirty-fourth and final Viceroy of India.

\tMountbatten arrived in New Delhi on March 22, 1947, charged with winding up the British Empire in India. Confronted with mounting communal violence across Punjab and Bengal, an unworkable coalition inside the Interim Government, and the Muslim League's unbending demand for partition, Mountbatten abandoned earlier unity proposals and concluded that immediate territorial division was the only viable political alternative to outright civil war.

\tOn June 3, 1947, Mountbatten presented the historic partition blueprint, known as the Mountbatten Plan (Mountbatten Yojna) or the 3rd June Plan (3 June chi Yojna). The plan advanced the date of power transfer from June 1948 to August 15, 1947, dividing British India into two sovereign dominions: India and Pakistan. It provided that the provincial legislative assemblies of Bengal and Punjab would meet in two sections representing Muslim-majority and non-Muslim-majority districts to vote on whether the provinces should be partitioned.

\tA referendum was mandated for the North-West Frontier Province (NWFP) and the Sylhet district of Assam. Khan Abdul Ghaffar Khan and the Khudai Khidmatgars boycotted the NWFP referendum, lamenting that the Congress leadership had thrown them "to the wolves". Both the Congress Working Committee and the All India Muslim League officially ratified the partition scheme.

\tTo delineate the new international boundaries, the British government appointed two boundary commissions on June 30, 1947, for Bengal and Punjab, chaired by British barrister Sir Cyril Radcliffe. The Radcliffe Line (Radcliffe Resha) demarcated the borders based on population distribution and contiguous territory, cutting through road networks, irrigation systems, and ancestral villages. The publication of the Radcliffe Award on August 17, 1947, unleashed one of the largest and bloodiest mass migrations in human history: over fifteen million people crossed the new frontiers, and communal massacres claimed hundreds of thousands of civilian lives across Punjab, Bengal, and Delhi.`,

28: `\tBased on the Mountbatten Plan, the British Parliament enacted the Indian Independence Act of 1947 (Bharatiya Swatantryacha Kayda 1947) on July 18, 1947. The act ended British imperial rule and established two independent dominions on August 15, 1947. British paramountcy over the princely states lapsed under Section 7, releasing more than five hundred and sixty-five native states from treaty obligations and giving them the option to accede to India, accede to Pakistan, or remain sovereign.

\tThrough the diplomacy of Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel and V. P. Menon, the princely states signed the Instrument of Accession (Vilinkaran Kararnama), integrating into the Indian Union, while Junagadh, Hyderabad, and Jammu and Kashmir required subsequent political and military resolution.

\tOn the midnight of August 14–15, 1947, the Constituent Assembly met in the Constitution Hall in New Delhi. Jawaharlal Nehru delivered his historic "Tryst with Destiny" address (Bhagyashri Sobat Karar), proclaiming the birth of a free nation.

\tPakistan celebrated independence on August 14 under Governor-General Muhammad Ali Jinnah in Karachi, while India celebrated independence on August 15, 1947, with Lord Mountbatten serving as the first Governor-General of the Dominion of India and Jawaharlal Nehru sworn in as the first Prime Minister.

\tWhen the Indian Independence Act of 1947 ended British paramountcy (Sarvabhaumatva), over 565 princely states (Sansthane) legally regained sovereign independence, threatening the balkanization of the subcontinent. The interim government formed the States Department (Sansthan Vibhag) on June 27, 1947, headed by Home Minister Sardar Vallabhbhai Patel, with V. P. Menon serving as Secretary. Patel pursued a firm policy combining patriotic appeals, royal privileges, and implicit military coercion. He persuaded the rulers to surrender only three subjects to the central dominion: Defense, Foreign Affairs, and Communications.

\tRulers signed two official instruments: the Standstill Agreement (Yathasthiti Karar) to preserve existing administrative arrangements, and the Instrument of Accession (Vilinkaran Kararnama). By August 15, 1947, almost all princely states whose territories fell within the Indian perimeter had acceded, except Junagadh, Hyderabad, and Jammu and Kashmir.

\tJunagadh was a small coastal principality in southwestern Gujarat with an overwhelming Hindu-majority population ruled by a Muslim Nawab, Muhammad Mahabat Khan III. On August 15, 1947, the Nawab unexpectedly announced accession to the Dominion of Pakistan, despite lacking contiguous land borders. The local population revolted, and prominent Gujarati leaders formed a provisional liberation government named the Arzi Hukumat (Arzi Hukumat) under Samaldas Gandhi.

\tWhen Indian troops surrounded the state and severed trade routes, the Nawab fled to Karachi with his family and treasury. In November 1947, Indian civil administrators assumed control of the state at the Dewan Shah Nawaz Bhutto's request. A public plebiscite (Sarvajanik Kaul / Matdan) held in February 1948 resulted in over ninety-nine percent of voters choosing merger with the Indian Union.`,

29: `\tJammu and Kashmir presented an inverted demographic problem: a predominantly Muslim population ruled by a Hindu Dogra monarch, Maharaja Hari Singh. The Maharaja sought to remain independent and signed a Standstill Agreement with Pakistan, which India refused without further negotiations. On October 22, 1947, Pakistan violated the agreement by launching Operation Gulmarg (Operation Gulmarg), an armed tribal invasion of Pashtun tribesmen (Afridi and Mehsud Pathans) led by Pakistani military officers, which advanced rapidly toward Srinagar, capturing Muzaffarabad and Baramulla. In panic, Maharaja Hari Singh appealed to New Delhi for immediate military intervention.

\tSardar Patel and V. P. Menon insisted on accession prior to deployment. On October 26, 1947, Hari Singh signed the formal Instrument of Accession (Vilinkaran Kararnama) at Jammu. On the morning of October 27, 1947, the Indian Air Force airlifted the 1st Sikh Regiment under Lieutenant Colonel Dewan Ranjit Rai into Srinagar airfield, securing the valley and driving back the raiders. Sheikh Abdullah, leader of the National Conference, supported the accession and was appointed head of the emergency administration.

\tHyderabad was the largest, wealthiest princely state in central India, ruled by the seventh Nizam, Mir Osman Ali Khan. The Nizam insisted on sovereign independence, supported by a militant communal paramilitary force named the Razakars (Razakar), led by Kasim Razvi. The Razakars terrorized the majority Hindu population and suppressed the peasant-led Telangana Rebellion (Telangana Shetkari Uthav). Swami Ramanand Tirtha led the Hyderabad State Congress (Hyderabad State Congress), demanding full democratic integration with India.

\tAfter the Nizam violated the November 1947 Standstill Agreement by purchasing foreign arms and attempting an alliance with Portugal over Goa, Sardar Patel ordered military intervention. On September 13, 1948, the Indian Army launched Operation Polo (Operation Polo), popularly known as the Police Action (Police Action), under Major General J. N. Chaudhuri. Within 108 hours, the Hyderabad State forces under Major General El Edroos surrendered on September 17, 1948. The Nizam signed the accession agreement, and Hyderabad formally merged into the Indian Republic.

\tFollowing national independence, the primary task of political reconstruction was framing a comprehensive constitution. The Constituent Assembly (Ghatanaparishad) formed several key committees to examine administrative, executive, and legal structures. On August 29, 1947, the assembly appointed the Drafting Committee (Mutsadda Samiti) comprising seven eminent members, electing Dr. Bhimrao Ramji Ambedkar as its Chairman (Adhyaksh). As the chief architect of the Indian Constitution, Dr. Ambedkar meticulously examined legal precedents across international constitutions, synthesizing fundamental rights, directive principles, and parliamentary democracy.`,

30: `\tSir B. N. Rau served as the Constitutional Adviser (Ghatanatmak Sallagar), preparing the initial working draft, while S. N. Mukherjee functioned as the chief draftsman. The assembly held eleven sessions over two years, eleven months, and eighteen days, deliberating through clause-by-clause readings and debating over two thousand amendments. On November 26, 1949, the Constituent Assembly formally adopted the Constitution of India (Bharatiya Samvidhan), a landmark date now commemorated as Constitution Day (Samvidhan Divas).

\tThe comprehensive document came into full legal effect on January 26, 1950, chosen deliberately to honor the twentieth anniversary of the Purna Swaraj declaration at the 1929 Lahore Congress session. Dr. Rajendra Prasad was sworn in as the first President of the Republic of India (Rashtrapati), replacing the British monarch as head of state and permanently ending India's dominion status under the 1947 Act. The national seal adopted the Lion Capital of Ashoka from Sarnath, featuring the Upanishadic motto "Satyameva Jayate" (Truth Alone Triumphs), while Rabindranath Tagore's Jana Gana Mana and Bankim Chandra Chatterjee's Vande Mataram were adopted as the National Anthem and National Song respectively.

\tThe political unification of the subcontinent remained incomplete until foreign European colonial enclaves were fully liquidated and integrated. The French held five commercial settlements (Vashahati): Chandernagore, Pondicherry, Karikal, Mahe, and Yanam. Through sustained diplomatic negotiations, France held a municipal plebiscite in Chandernagore in 1949, ceding it to India in May 1950. On November 1, 1954, following popular local agitations led by V. Subbiah, the French government surrendered administrative control of the remaining four enclaves to India via de facto transfer, formalized through a de jure treaty ratified in August 1962, consolidating them into the Union Territory of Puducherry.

\tPortugal, governed by the authoritarian dictator Antonio de Oliveira Salazar, adamantly refused to negotiate the handover of Portuguese India (Estado da India). Local freedom fighters organized the Goa Liberation Movement (Goa Mukti Sangram), supported by prominent leaders like Ram Manohar Lohia, Dr. Tristao de Braganza Cunha, and the Azad Gomantak Dal under Vishwanath Lawande. In July and August 1954, volunteers from the Rashtriya Swayamsevak Sangh, Praja Socialist Party, and Azad Gomantak Dal liberated the inland enclaves of Dadra and Nagar Haveli, forming a local self-governing administration until official Indian integration in 1961.

\tIn August 1955, thousands of unarmed Indian satyagrahis marched into Goa, where Portuguese police opened fire, killing over twenty peaceful protesters and injuring hundreds. Prime Minister Jawaharlal Nehru authorized decisive military action. On December 18, 1961, the Indian Armed Forces launched Operation Vijay, sending infantry and naval vessels under Major General K. P. Candeth into Goa, Daman, and Diu. After thirty-six hours, Portuguese Governor-General Manuel Antonio Vassalo e Silva signed the formal instrument of unconditional surrender on December 19, 1961. The four-hundred-and-fifty-one-year-old Portuguese colonial presence in India came to a final end.`,

};
